    "body": "Nearly half of manufacturing HR professionals say they struggle with job candidates using AI to misrepresent themselves, according to Criteria’s 2026–2027 Hiring Benchmark Report . At the same time, more than two-thirds are using AI in their own recruiting or hiring processes.\nWhat’s Related\nThe survey found that 45% of manufacturing respondents identified AI misrepresentation as a hiring challenge, compared with 41% across all industries. Manufacturing ranked behind technology (60%) and professional services (47%).\nCriteria surveyed 1,027 U.S. hiring professionals in summer 2026. Manufacturing accounted for 11% of respondents, whose roles included human resources, hiring management, and business ownership.\nThe findings describe a hiring process in which AI is becoming common on both sides of the application. Employers use it to evaluate candidates, while many say applicants use it to present an inaccurate picture of themselves.\nRELATED STORIES: Gartner Warns AI-Driven Hiring Freeze Could Backfire by 2030 | New Study Says AI Helps Workers and Creates More Warehouse Roles\nEmployers unsure what to believe\nAcross all industries, 24% of respondents said they had lost confidence in résumés as a reliable way to judge candidates. Another 35% said their current hiring process could not reliably detect when candidates used AI to misrepresent themselves.\nThese findings cover all industries surveyed. They show employers’ concerns, but don’t tell us how many applicants actually misled them.\nFinding qualified candidates remains the larger challenge. Among manufacturing respondents, 47% reported persistent skills shortages, and 53% said hiring was more difficult than a year earlier.\nDespite those difficulties, 74% of manufacturing respondents expected to hire more in the coming year. That was higher than the 64% reported across all industries.\nManufacturers also using AI to hire\nManufacturing had the second-highest reported use of AI in recruiting or hiring, at 69%, behind technology’s 87%. Across the full survey, 56% said they used AI in those processes.\nThe report found AI being used for tasks ranging from screening applications to scheduling and interviews. Across all respondents, 46% reported using AI for screening, while 35% used it for assessments and 35% for scheduling.\nEmployers also identified obstacles to using the technology themselves. Accuracy was the most common concern, cited by 42% of respondents, followed by data privacy at 33%.\nEven with those concerns, 72% of manufacturing respondents were optimistic about AI’s role in improving hiring, and 64% were confident it would improve the quality of their hiring decisions.",
